<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Describing Relationships</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>
    As we continue to study patterns and graphs we want to describe relationships between sets.  We 
    will describe these relationships using words, tables (numerically), graphs,
     and equations.  We will choose the method of description based on the 
     information we want to convey about the relationship.
  </p>

  </introduction>
  
  <example xml:id="exA-TS">
  <title>Temperature scales</title>
  
  
    <introduction>
      <p>
        One relationship we can consider is how temperature measured on the Celsius
         scale compares to temperature measured on the Fahrenheit scale.  
         The table below provides a numerical representation between these two scales.  
      </p>
      <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor" left="minor">
          <row>
            <cell>Temperature in degrees Celsius</cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>20</cell>
            <cell>30</cell>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>50</cell>
            <cell>60</cell>
            <cell>70</cell>
            <cell>80</cell>
            <cell>90</cell>
            <cell>100</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Temperature in degrees Fahrenheit</cell>
            <cell>32</cell>
            <cell>50</cell>
            <cell>68</cell>
            <cell>86</cell>
            <cell>104</cell>
            <cell>122</cell>
            <cell>140</cell>
            <cell>158</cell>
            <cell>176</cell>
            <cell>194</cell>
            <cell>212</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Look at the two rows, what do we notice about the data in each row?   
        How might we use this information to describe the relationship between 
        the two temperature scales?  

      </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
       In this case we write <m>F=\frac{9}{5}C+32</m>.  (We'll discuss how to find the equations in 
      more detail in later lessons.)  Notice here that <m>F</m> serves as a variable representing the 
      temperature in degrees Fahrenheit and <m>C</m> a variable for the temperature in degrees Celsius.  
      We can use the equation to find the relationship between temperature scales for additional points.
       
        </p>
      

    <task workspace="1in">
      <p>
       Find the temperature in degrees Fahrenheit at <m>25^{\circ}C</m>.
      </p> 
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <p>
        Find the temperature in degrees Fahrenheit at <m>105^{\circ}C</m>.
      </p>
    </task>
    </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        To consider the graphical representation of a relationship, we place the input variable 
        along the horizontal (independent) axis and the output variable along the 
        vertical (dependent) axis.  Choose an appropriate scale and plot our temperature data below.
      </p>
      <image source="BlankGrid10.png" width="50%">
        <description><p>Blank Grid for graphing</p></description>
      </image>
      
      </statement>
    </task>

    
    
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exA-TB">
  <title> Height of a tennis ball </title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A tennis ball is dropped and allowed to bounce twice. Its height is measured over time. A graphical representation of this relationship is given below.
      </p>
      <image source="tennisball.png" width="50%">
        <description><p>
        A graph giving the height of a tennis ball in terms of time.  The curve fall a parabolic arc from (0,6) to (3,0), then another arc from (3,0) to (6,0) reaching a vertex at (4.5, 4.5). A final arc from (6,0) to (9,0) with a vertex at (7.5, 2.25)
        </p></description>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify the independent and dependent variables.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <alert>The domain is defined as the set of all input values for the relationship.</alert>
          What is the domain for this relationship.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <alert>The range is defined as the set of all output values for the relationship.</alert>
        What is the range for this relationship?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <alert>An intercept is where the graph intersects one of the axes.</alert>
        Determine the intercepts of this graph and explain their meaning with respect to the relationship.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the intervals on which the graph is increasing
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the intervals on which the graph is decreasing
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>
    
  <example xml:id="exA-RE">
    <introduction>
      <p>
       A company found that if it priced its product at $40 it would sell 120 items per week.
Experimenting with different prices they found that for every $5 increase in price they sold 12
fewer items each week. 
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Model the relationship between weekly demand for the product in terms
of its price using a numerical representation.
        </p>
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor" left="minor">
          <row>
            <cell>Price <m>(p)</m> in dollars</cell>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>50</cell>
            <cell>55</cell>
            <cell>60</cell>
            <cell>65</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Weekly demand <m>(d)</m> in items</cell>
            <cell>120</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The revenue a company earns selling products can be calculated by multiplying the price they
sell each item for by the number of items sold. Model the relationship between the weekly
revenue for the company in terms of the price it sells its products for numerically.
        </p>
        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor" left="minor">
          <row>
            <cell>Price <m>(p)</m> in dollars</cell>
            <cell>40</cell>
            <cell>45</cell>
            <cell>50</cell>
            <cell>55</cell>
            <cell>60</cell>
            <cell>65</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Weekly revenue <m>(R)</m> in dollars</cell>
            <cell><m>120\dcot40=4800</m></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using Desmos create a graphical representation for the weekly revenue in terms of price.
        </p>
      </statement>
        </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Revenue can be described in terms of price by the equation <m>R=-2.4p^2+216p</m>.  Graph this equation with your data on Desmos.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the independent variable for this relationship?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the dependent variable for this relationship?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the intercepts of the graph.  Explain their meaning within the context of the relationship.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is a reasonable domain for this relationship? Why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is a reasonable range for this relationship? Why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>
</worksheet>